{"records":[{"id":"65k3y4smr5","title":"ATG5 attenuates inflammatory signaling in embryonic stem cells to control differentiation","description":"Attenuated inflammatory responses to both inflammation-related cytokines and infectious agents are recognized as a unique property of embryonic stem cells (ESCs). Although an increasing number of studies have reported this attenuation, the underlying molecular mechanisms are still unclear. Moreover, whether the attenuated inflammatory status is involved in ESC differentiation is completely unknown. Here, we found that the autophagy-related protein ATG5 is essential for both the attenuated inflammatory response and the differentiation of ESCs, and that the attenuation of inflammatory signaling is required for ESC differentiation. Mechanistically, ATG5 promotes 26S proteasome-mediated degradation of the SCF (SKP1-cullin-1-F-box-protein) E3 ubiquitin ligase β-TrCP1 (beta-transducin repeat containing E3 ubiquitin protein ligase 1), which is a well-known positive regulator of NF-κB signaling and the inflammatory response. Another SCF E3 ubiquitin ligase, FBXW7 (F-box and WD repeat domain containing 7), which is recruited by ATG5, was found to be the E3 ubiquitin ligase responsible for the ubiquitination and degradation of β-TrCP1. Moreover, the differentiation defects observed in ATG5-depleted ESCs are due to the accumulation of β-TrCP1 and the related hyperactivation of NF-κB signaling, as loss of β-TrCP1 and inhibition of NF-κB signaling rescued the differentiation defects in ATG5-depleted ESCs. Therefore, this study reveals a previously uncharacterized mechanism employed by ESCs to maintain the inflammatory response in an attenuated state and further provides novel insight into the biological roles of ATG5, especially in the control of the inflammatory response and differentiation of ESCs.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-22T08:57:47.555Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65k3y4smr5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65k3y4smr5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"sheng","last_name":"li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jin","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bo-Wen","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lu","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ying-Cui","last_name":"Wan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bei-Bei","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nan","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qian-Ru","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Juan","last_name":"Fan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jia-Ni","last_name":"Shang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rui","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chen-Ge","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yan","last_name":"Xi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Su","last_name":"Chen","orcid":"0000-0001-8804-8989"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"ZR2021QC181"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"222300420028"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82273067"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"81972650"}],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ph2xvpxs2y","title":"DNA damage promotes TMPRSS2-ERG oncoprotein destruction and prostate cancer suppression via signaling converged by GSK3β and WEE1. Hong et al","description":"TMPRSS2-ERG gene fusion occurs in approximately 50% of prostate cancer (PCa) and the fusion product is a key driver of prostate oncogenesis. However, how to leverage cellular signaling to ablate TMPRSS2-ERG oncoprotein for PCa treatment remains elusive. Here, we demonstrate that DNA damage induces proteasomal degradation of wild-type ERG and TMPRSS2-ERG oncoprotein through ERG threonine-187 and tyrosine-190 phosphorylation mediated by GSK3β and WEE1, respectively. The dual phosphorylation triggers ERG recognition and degradation by the E3 ubiquitin ligase FBW7 in a manner independent of a canonical degron. DNA damage-induced TMPRSS2-ERG degradation was abolished by cancer-associated PTEN deletion or GSK3β inactivation. Blockade of DNA damage-induced TMPRSS2-ERG oncoprotein degradation causes chemotherapy-resistant growth of fusion-positive PCa cells in culture and in mice. Our findings uncover a previously unrecognized TMPRSS2-ERG protein destruction mechanism and demonstrate that intact PTEN and GSK3β signaling are essential for effective targeting of ERG protein by genotoxic therapeutics in fusion-positive PCa.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-18T22:45:00.674Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ph2xvpxs2y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ph2xvpxs2y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Haojie","last_name":"Huang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9d5499gb8x","title":"In Vivo CRISPR Screens Identify E3 Ligase Cop1 as a Modulator of Macrophage Infiltration and Cancer Immunotherapy Target","description":"Despite remarkable clinical efficacy of immune checkpoint blockade (ICB) in cancer treatment, ICB benefits in triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC) remain limited. Through pooled in vivo CRISPR knockout (KO) screens in syngeneic TNBC mouse models, we found that deletion of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells decreases the secretion of macrophage-associated chemokines, reduces tumor macrophage infiltration, enhances anti-tumor immunity, and strengthens ICB response. Transcriptomics, epigenomics, and proteomics analyses revealed that Cop1 functions through proteasomal degradation of the C/ebpδ protein. Cop1 substrate Trib2 functions as a scaffold linking Cop1 and C/ebpδ, which leads to polyubiquitination of C/ebpδ. In addition, deletion of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells stabilizes C/ebpδ to suppress the expression of macrophage chemoattractant genes. Our integrated approach implicates Cop1 as a target for improving cancer immunotherapy efficacy in TNBC by regulating chemokine secretion and macrophage infiltration in the tumor microenvironment.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-24T14:43:28.246Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9d5499gb8x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9d5499gb8x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"xiaoqing","last_name":"wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zmyr2jj8v6","title":"Exportin 1 pathway inhibition up-regulates nuclear and mitochondrial localization of dengue virus non-structural protein 3 ","description":"Supplementary data showing controls for exportin 1 (XPO1)-dependent nuclear export inhibition with Leptomycin B and Selinexor. Representative figures show nuclear retention of the cellular protein Cyclin B1, which is exported from the nucleus via XPO1, indicating that the drugs Leptomycin B and Selinexor specifically inhibit the XPO1 nuclear export pathway.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-22T10:37:18.988Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zmyr2jj8v6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zmyr2jj8v6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Selvin","last_name":"Palacios","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rosa M","last_name":"del Angel","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mitochondrial Transport","Nuclear Export","Dengue Virus","Exportin 1 Protein"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mphtn2wxwm","title":"LINF_260012000","description":"E3 ubiquitin-protein ligase; Leishmania infantum (strain JPCM5)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-31T13:36:13.846Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mphtn2wxwm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mphtn2wxwm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose M.","last_name":"Requena","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","grant_id":"PID2020-117916RB-I00/AEI/10.13039/501100011033"}],"categories":["Gene","Protein","Leishmania"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s7thkp9z4r","title":"The CBL-LSD1-CXCL8 Axis Regulates Methionine Metabolism in Glioma","description":"We found that CXCL8 expression was significantly increased in a methionine restricted environment due to the upregulation of H3K4 methylation. Mechanistically, the E3 ubiquitin ligase CBL mediated the ubiquitinated degradation of histone demethylase LSD1, reducing LSD1 protein stability and enhancing H3K4me1 modification of the CXCL8 gene.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-24T09:21:30.923Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s7thkp9z4r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s7thkp9z4r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"jie","last_name":"chang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Metabolism","Glioma","Methionine"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"yc2c8t7y3r","title":"LINF_240021600","description":"RING-box E3 ubiquitin ligase; Leishmania infantum (strain JPCM5)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-05T12:32:03.515Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yc2c8t7y3r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yc2c8t7y3r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose M.","last_name":"Requena","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","grant_id":"PID2020-117916RB-I00/AEI/10.13039/501100011033"}],"categories":["Gene","Protein","Leishmania"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gtwgyd5gzs","title":"LINF_210005300","description":"RING-box E3 ubiquitin ligase; Leishmania infantum (strain JPCM5)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-06-25T00:12:47.679Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gtwgyd5gzs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gtwgyd5gzs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose M.","last_name":"Requena","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","grant_id":"PID2020-117916RB-I00/AEI/10.13039/501100011033"}],"categories":["Gene","Protein","Leishmania"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xhwbvfh74g","title":"LINF_160011800","description":"E3 ubiquitin-protein ligase; Leishmania infantum (strain JPCM5)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-22T09:29:19.14Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xhwbvfh74g","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xhwbvfh74g"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose M.","last_name":"Requena","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","grant_id":"PID2020-117916RB-I00/AEI/10.13039/501100011033"}],"categories":["Gene","Protein","Leishmania"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vgrn337d3x","title":"LINF_320046500","description":"E3 ubiquitin-protein ligase domain-containing protein; Leishmania infantum (strain JPCM5)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-14T19:04:51.882Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vgrn337d3x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vgrn337d3x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose M.","last_name":"Requena","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","grant_id":"PID2020-117916RB-I00/AEI/10.13039/501100011033"}],"categories":["Gene","Protein","Leishmania"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":121976,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":141,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":121976,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":121513,"restricted":60,"embargoed":403},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2053},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1401},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":557},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":440},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":418},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":382},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":381},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":337},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":299},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":253}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":119413},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1889},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":176},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":127},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":69},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":56},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":52},{"i